> Is it? Or is it to allow the player to imagine themselves in a
> particular fantasy role? 

No, sir.  Not given the historical ancestry of Nethack, nor the current
state of gameplay.  It's randomized dungeon-crawl hack n' slash all the
way from the word "go", with three FedEx quests and one "Kill the Baddie"
stuck on to serve as an apology for plot.  (Not that there's anything
inherently wrong with that, but we need to be very clear what we're 
dealing with here.)  The original Rogue had one role, '@'; the Nethack roles 
were intended to add some variety to the process of slaying the entire dungeon.

There are, indeed, a few token bits of flavor text added (the samurai's
japanese wording, the Valkyrie's "Velkommen!", etc.), but other than
that, the whole concept of "roleplaying" exists not at all in
Nethack-the-game; it may exist in a few RPG-starved Nethack players'
minds, but confusing Nethack with any sort of RPG would be a tragic
error.

> I say: keep all the classes, even if they result in identical play,
> because they add flavour. They are already differentiated
> stylistically. More differentiation might be a good thing, but it
> isn't mandatory.

I agree with the base premise: keep all the classes.  There's a nice
pleasing roundness to "13", as well.  However, I also consider the lack
of differentiation in those classes to be a core design flaw in Nethack
(the Dev Team appears to agree with me on this, oddly enough), and as
such is an excellent point for changes to be focused on.

Nothing is "mandatory"; it's just a game, and the game can be played
as-is.  But the game would be far better with 13 different roles, rather
than essentially 1 role with 13 slightly different bits of flavor.

